// `let` BINDS ONE BARE IDENTIFIER. THERE ARE NO `let` PATTERNS.
//
// Same production, same comment, other half:
//   let_stmt = "let" [ "mut" ] identifier [ ':' type ] '=' expression ';' ;
//   "the binding is a bare identifier — `let` patterns do not exist."
//
// A tuple is read with `.0`/`.1`, which this language does have and which
// tests/04_tuples.pd and the type-alias e2e test execute. `match` is where
// patterns live; `let` is not a pattern position.
//
// SEPARATE FILE FROM let_needs_an_initializer.pd deliberately: the two halves are
// refused by DIFFERENT branches of the parser and produce different diagnostics
// (one wanted `=`, this one wanted a variable name), so one fixture pinning both
// would stay green if either half regressed. Discovered the same way, from the
// same `#[ignore]`, which demanded `let (from, to, weight) = graph[i];`.
fn main() {
    let e: (i64, i64) = (1, 2);
    let (a, b) = e;
    print_int(a);
    print_int(b);
}
